**Reconciliation and diffing.** When state changes, React re-renders the component to produce a new virtual DOM (element tree), compares it with the previous tree and applies the minimum DOM changes. The diff uses heuristics to stay near `O(n)`: elements of different types are replaced (the subtree is rebuilt), same-type elements are updated in place, and lists use `key` to match items between renders. Stable, unique keys (not array indexes for reorderable lists) prevent state mix-ups and needless remounts. Fiber, the current engine, splits work into units that can be paused, which enables concurrent rendering and priorities.

**Rules of hooks.** Call hooks only at the top level (not in loops, conditions or nested functions), and only from function components or other custom hooks, because React identifies a hook by its call order. A custom hook is a function named `useSomething` that calls other hooks to share stateful logic; each use has its own state.

**Basic `useState` and `useEffect` (conceptual).**

```js
let hooks = [], i = 0;
function useState(init) {
  const idx = i;
  hooks[idx] = hooks[idx] ?? init;
  const set = v => { hooks[idx] = v; rerender(); };
  i++;
  return [hooks[idx], set];
}
function useEffect(fn, deps) {
  const idx = i++;
  const old = hooks[idx];
  const changed = !old || !deps || deps.some((d, k) => d !== old.deps[k]);
  if (changed) { old?.cleanup?.(); hooks[idx] = { deps, cleanup: fn() }; }
}
// rerender() resets i = 0 and calls the component again
```

State lives in an array indexed by call order, which is exactly why the rules of hooks exist.

**Closures.** A function keeps access to the variables of the scope where it was created. Stale closures in effects and handlers (capturing old state) are a classic hook bug, solved with correct dependency arrays or functional updates (`setX(x => x + 1)`).

**`let` vs `var`.** `var` is function-scoped and hoisted (initialised to `undefined`); `let` is block-scoped and in the temporal dead zone until declared. The classic loop-closure bug with `var` is fixed by `let`, which creates a binding per iteration.

**Tailwind vs CSS-in-JS.** Tailwind is utility classes with build-time CSS, small output and no runtime cost; styles live in markup. CSS-in-JS (styled-components, Emotion) gives dynamic, component-scoped styles in JS but adds runtime cost and complicates SSR (newer zero-runtime options exist).

**SSR.** The server renders HTML for fast first paint and SEO; the client then hydrates it to make it interactive. The costs are server load and hydration mismatches; frameworks like Next.js also offer static generation and streaming.
